<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">The Weald</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Weald is an area of south-east <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a> between the North and South Downs, covering parts of Kent, Sussex, Surrey and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hampshire">Hampshire</a>. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anglo-saxons">Anglo-Saxon</a> times it was a great forest, Andredesweald, that separated the South <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saxons">Saxons</a> from their neighbours and was used mainly for seasonal pasture. From Roman times to the 18th century it was also an important iron-producing district.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/the-weald.jpg" alt="The Weald" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Rjgibb · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:WealdfromDowns.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>The Weald is the eroded remains of an anticline, a dome of layered Lower Cretaceous rocks worn down to expose sandstone ridges and clay valleys. Its hard central sandstones form the High Weald, the softer sandstones and clays of its edges the gentler Low Weald, and the Greensand Ridge to the north and west holds its highest points. It stretches about 85 miles from west to east and 30 miles from north to south, and its streams feed the Mole, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/river-medway">Medway</a>, Stour, Rother, Cuckmere, Ouse, Adur and Arun. The name comes from the Old English weald, forest.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Wealden sandstones contain ironstone, and with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/timber">timber</a> for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charcoal">charcoal</a> the area became the centre of the Wealden iron industry; of the 33 iron mines listed on the Ordnance Survey Map of Roman Britain, 67% lie in the Weald. By the later 16th century as many as one hundred furnaces and forges were at work. The 9th-century <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anglo-saxon-chronicle">Anglo-Saxon Chronicle</a> gives the forest a length of 120 miles or more and tells how in 477 Ælle drove the Britons into the wood called Andred'sley.</p><p>Population grew between 800 and 1300 as woodland was cleared into small irregular fields, and seasonal pasture settlements, the folds of Sussex and the dens of Kent, became permanent by the end of the 12th century. In 1216 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/archery">archers</a> of the Weald under William of Cassingham ambushed the French army of Prince Louis near Lewes. In 1264 Wealden archers harried the army of King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/henry-iii-of-england">Henry III of England</a>, who ordered captured archers executed and beheaded 300 after a local man shot his cook.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Woodland still covers 23% of the Weald, one of the highest shares in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a>. Parts lie in the South Downs National Park and the Surrey Hills and High Weald Areas of Outstanding Natural Beauty, and Ashdown Forest, a Norman royal hunting forest, is said to be the largest remaining part of Andredesweald. The largest towns include Horsham, Tunbridge Wells and Tonbridge.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Weald” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Settlement was hemmed in by the dense <b>Weald</b> forest, which isolated the kingdom from its neighbours.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-sussex">Kingdom of Sussex</a></li></ul></section>
